<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Isabela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a>–<a href="/years/1498">1498</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Isabela was the first Spanish town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, in the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, during his second voyage at the start of 1494. Disease, hunger and quarrels plagued the settlers, and it was abandoned within a few years in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>. Its remains are an archaeological park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-isabela.jpg" alt="La Isabela" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heralder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Banner_of_the_Crown_of_Castille_(Habsburg_Style).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies in Puerto Plata Province, 42 km west of the city of Puerto Plata, beside the village of El Castillo. Columbus named it after Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a>. He had come back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> with seventeen ships and more than a thousand men, among them sailors, soldiers, carpenters, stonemasons, priests and nobles, to find that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>, the settlement left on his first voyage, had been destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>. The new town was meant as a base for the search for precious metals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Mass was said on 6 January 1494. The town had about 200 thatched huts, a plaza, a church, storerooms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s stone house and arsenal. The settlers brought pigs, horses, wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> and guns, and with them rats and the first influenza epidemic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> of the nearby mountains supplied much of their food, and from March 1494 Spaniards searched the mountains with them for gold, finding small amounts. A hurricane in June 1495 sank several ships, among them the flagship Marie-Galante.</p><p>Hunger and disease led to mutiny: Bernal de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and a group of settlers tried to seize ships and sail home. In March 1495 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>, allied with the cacique Guacanagari, marched against the other caciques and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a>, whom he held responsible for the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>. When Columbus sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 10 March 1496, only 630 Christians remained, most of them sick, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> had lost two thirds of their number. He chose to move the colony to Nueva Isabela, soon renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Graves at the site hold at least one European woman and people of African origin, though the records name neither. Cannon barrels and anchors of the period have been found in the bay, where river silt covers whatever remains of the wrecks. The area is now a National Historic Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Isabela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Columbus founded <b>La Isabela</b> on the north coast of Hispaniola in 1493, and Santo Domingo was established on the south coast in 1496-98.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-santo-domingo">Captaincy General of Santo Domingo</a></li></ul></section>
